{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-143698" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Suppose that for the angles $u$ and $v,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡u=\\frac{3}{5},\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡v=\\frac{8}{17},\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡v=-\\frac{15}{17}.\n$$\n\nGiven that $u$ lies in the first quadrant, find the value of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u-v).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042307/state.json" "file_sha256" "91de0b72952f6605dfeaab5b348a2225fed004b595bcbad5398beb49a5f2e3ef"} {"question" "q-143698" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, recall the difference formula for sine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u-v)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v-\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\n$$\n\nWe know the values of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡u$, $\\operatorname{sin}⁡v$, and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡v.$ To find the missing value of $\\operatorname{cos}⁡u$, we can use the Pythagorean identity:\n\n$$\n{sin}^{2}⁡u+{cos}^{2}⁡u=1\\,⟹\\,{cos}^{2}⁡u=1-{sin}^{2}⁡u\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values into the Pythagorean identity, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{cos}^{2}⁡u & =1-{sin}^{2}⁡u \\\\  & =1-{(\\frac{3}{5})}^{2} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{{3}^{2}}{{5}^{2}} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{9}{25} \\\\  & =\\frac{25}{25}-\\frac{9}{25} \\\\  & =\\frac{16}{25}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡u & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{16}{25}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{4}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that $u$ lies in the first quadrant, where cosine is positive. So, we disregard the negative value, and we have \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡u=\\frac{4}{5}.\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the difference formula, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u-v) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v-\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v \\\\  & =(\\frac{3}{5})(-\\frac{15}{17})-(\\frac{4}{5})(\\frac{8}{17}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{45}{85}-\\frac{32}{85} \\\\  & =-\\frac{77}{85}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042307/state.json" "file_sha256" "91de0b72952f6605dfeaab5b348a2225fed004b595bcbad5398beb49a5f2e3ef"} {"question" "q-143698" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042307/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "769f6b658de1e87837e89c0a286c34b86d9a2f842100ee4154533f402cb568f9"} {"question" "q-143698" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042307/state.json" "file_sha256" "91de0b72952f6605dfeaab5b348a2225fed004b595bcbad5398beb49a5f2e3ef"} {"question" "q-143698" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-\\frac{36}{85}"] ["math" "-\\frac{43}{85}"] ["math" "-\\frac{77}{85}"] ["math" "\\frac{13}{85}"] ["math" "\\frac{84}{85}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042307/state.json" "file_sha256" "91de0b72952f6605dfeaab5b348a2225fed004b595bcbad5398beb49a5f2e3ef"} {"question" "q-143698" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-\\frac{77}{85}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042307/state.json" "file_sha256" "91de0b72952f6605dfeaab5b348a2225fed004b595bcbad5398beb49a5f2e3ef"} {"question" "q-326363" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Suppose that for the angles $u$ and $v,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡u=\\frac{8}{17},\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡u=\\frac{15}{17},\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡v=\\frac{3}{5}.\n$$\n\nIf $v$ lies in the first quadrant, then $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u-v)=${{field-1}}." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042307/state.json" "file_sha256" "91de0b72952f6605dfeaab5b348a2225fed004b595bcbad5398beb49a5f2e3ef"} {"question" "q-326363" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, recall the difference formula for sine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u-v)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v-\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\n$$\n\nWe know the values of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡u$, $\\operatorname{cos}⁡u$, and $\\operatorname{sin}⁡v.$ To find the missing value of $\\operatorname{cos}⁡v$, we can use the Pythagorean identity:\n\n$$\n{sin}^{2}⁡v+{cos}^{2}⁡v=1\\,⟹\\,{cos}^{2}⁡v=1-{sin}^{2}⁡v\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values into the Pythagorean identity, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{cos}^{2}⁡v & =1-{(\\frac{3}{5})}^{2} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{{3}^{2}}{{5}^{2}} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{9}{25} \\\\  & =\\frac{16}{25}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡v & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{16}{25}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{4}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that $v$ lies in the first quadrant, where cosine is positive. So, we disregard the negative value, and we have \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡v=\\frac{4}{5}.\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the difference formula as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u-v) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v-\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v \\\\  & =(\\frac{8}{17})(\\frac{4}{5})-(\\frac{15}{17})(\\frac{3}{5}) \\\\  & =\\frac{32}{85}-\\frac{45}{85} \\\\  & =-\\frac{13}{85}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042307/state.json" "file_sha256" "91de0b72952f6605dfeaab5b348a2225fed004b595bcbad5398beb49a5f2e3ef"} {"question" "q-326363" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042307/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "769f6b658de1e87837e89c0a286c34b86d9a2f842100ee4154533f402cb568f9"} {"question" "q-326363" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042307/state.json" "file_sha256" "91de0b72952f6605dfeaab5b348a2225fed004b595bcbad5398beb49a5f2e3ef"} {"question" "q-326363" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-\\frac{13}{85}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042307/state.json" "file_sha256" "91de0b72952f6605dfeaab5b348a2225fed004b595bcbad5398beb49a5f2e3ef"} {"question" "q-141357" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Suppose that for the angles $u$ and $v,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡u=\\frac{8}{17},\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡v=\\frac{4}{5},\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡v=\\frac{3}{5}.\n$$\n\nGiven that $u$ lies in the first quadrant, find the value of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u-v).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042307/state.json" "file_sha256" "91de0b72952f6605dfeaab5b348a2225fed004b595bcbad5398beb49a5f2e3ef"} {"question" "q-141357" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, recall the difference formula for sine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u-v)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v-\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\n$$\n\nWe know the values of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡u$, $\\operatorname{sin}⁡v$, and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡v.$ To find the missing value of $\\operatorname{cos}⁡u$, we can use the Pythagorean identity:\n\n$$\n{sin}^{2}⁡u+{cos}^{2}⁡u=1\\,⟹\\,{cos}^{2}⁡u=1-{sin}^{2}⁡u\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values into the Pythagorean identity, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{cos}^{2}⁡u & =1-{sin}^{2}⁡u \\\\  & =1-{(\\frac{8}{17})}^{2} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{{8}^{2}}{{17}^{2}} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{64}{289} \\\\  & =\\frac{289}{289}-\\frac{64}{289} \\\\  & =\\frac{225}{289}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡u & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{225}{289}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{15}{17}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that $u$ lies in the first quadrant, where cosine is positive. So, we disregard the negative value, and we have \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡u=\\frac{15}{17}.\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the difference formula, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u-v) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v-\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v \\\\  & =(\\frac{8}{17})(\\frac{3}{5})-(\\frac{15}{17})(\\frac{4}{5}) \\\\  & =\\frac{24}{85}-\\frac{60}{85} \\\\  & =-\\frac{36}{85}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042307/state.json" "file_sha256" "91de0b72952f6605dfeaab5b348a2225fed004b595bcbad5398beb49a5f2e3ef"} {"question" "q-141357" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042307/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "769f6b658de1e87837e89c0a286c34b86d9a2f842100ee4154533f402cb568f9"} {"question" "q-141357" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042307/state.json" "file_sha256" "91de0b72952f6605dfeaab5b348a2225fed004b595bcbad5398beb49a5f2e3ef"} {"question" "q-141357" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-\\frac{2}{3}"] ["math" "-\\frac{36}{85}"] ["math" "\\frac{12}{25}"] ["math" "\\frac{15}{17}"] ["math" "\\frac{84}{85}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042307/state.json" "file_sha256" "91de0b72952f6605dfeaab5b348a2225fed004b595bcbad5398beb49a5f2e3ef"} {"question" "q-141357" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-\\frac{36}{85}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042307/state.json" "file_sha256" "91de0b72952f6605dfeaab5b348a2225fed004b595bcbad5398beb49a5f2e3ef"} {"question" "q-274503" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Suppose that for the angles $u$ and $v,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡u=\\frac{3}{5},\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡u=-\\frac{4}{5},\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡v=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nIf $v$ lies in the first quadrant, then $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u+v)=${{field-1}}." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042307/state.json" "file_sha256" "91de0b72952f6605dfeaab5b348a2225fed004b595bcbad5398beb49a5f2e3ef"} {"question" "q-274503" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, recall the sum formula for sine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u+v)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v+\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\n$$\n\nWe know the values of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡u$, $\\operatorname{cos}⁡u$, and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡v.$ To find the missing value of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡v$, we can use the Pythagorean identity:\n\n$$\n{sin}^{2}⁡v+{cos}^{2}⁡v=1\\,⟹\\,{sin}^{2}⁡v=1-{cos}^{2}⁡v\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values into the Pythagorean identity, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡v & =1-{cos}^{2}⁡v \\\\  & =1-{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{{1}^{2}}{{2}^{2}} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{4}-\\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡v & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{3}{4}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that $v$ lies in the first quadrant, where sine is positive. So, we disregard the negative value, and we have \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡v=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the sum formula as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u+v) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v+\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v \\\\  & =(\\frac{3}{5})(\\frac{1}{2})+(-\\frac{4}{5})(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{10}-\\frac{4\\sqrt{3}}{10} \\\\  & =\\frac{3-4\\sqrt{3}}{10}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042307/state.json" "file_sha256" "91de0b72952f6605dfeaab5b348a2225fed004b595bcbad5398beb49a5f2e3ef"} {"question" "q-274503" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042307/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "769f6b658de1e87837e89c0a286c34b86d9a2f842100ee4154533f402cb568f9"} {"question" "q-274503" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042307/state.json" "file_sha256" "91de0b72952f6605dfeaab5b348a2225fed004b595bcbad5398beb49a5f2e3ef"} {"question" "q-274503" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{3-4\\sqrt{3}}{10}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042307/state.json" "file_sha256" "91de0b72952f6605dfeaab5b348a2225fed004b595bcbad5398beb49a5f2e3ef"} {"question" "q-119451" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Suppose that for the angles $u$ and $v,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡u=\\frac{\\sqrt{11}}{6},\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡v=\\frac{2}{7},\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡v=-\\frac{3\\sqrt{5}}{7}.\n$$\n\nGiven that $u$ lies in the first quadrant, and\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u+v)=\\frac{a-b\\sqrt{55}}{42},\n$$\n\ncalculate the value of $a+b.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042307/state.json" "file_sha256" "91de0b72952f6605dfeaab5b348a2225fed004b595bcbad5398beb49a5f2e3ef"} {"question" "q-119451" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, recall the sum formula for sine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u+v)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v+\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\n$$\n\nWe know the values of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡u$, $\\operatorname{sin}⁡v$, and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡v.$ To find the missing value of $\\operatorname{cos}⁡u$, we can use the Pythagorean identity:\n\n$$\n{sin}^{2}⁡u+{cos}^{2}⁡u=1\\,⟹\\,{cos}^{2}⁡u=1-{sin}^{2}⁡u\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values into the Pythagorean identity, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{cos}^{2}⁡u & =1-{sin}^{2}⁡u \\\\  & =1-{(\\frac{\\sqrt{11}}{6})}^{2} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{(\\sqrt{11})^{2}}{{6}^{2}} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{11}{36} \\\\  & =\\frac{36}{36}-\\frac{11}{36} \\\\  & =\\frac{36-11}{36} \\\\  & =\\frac{25}{36}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡u & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{25}{36}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{5}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that $u$ lies in the first quadrant, where cosine is positive. So, we disregard the negative value, and we have \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡u=\\frac{5}{6}.\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the sum formula, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u+v) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v+\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v \\\\  & =(\\frac{\\sqrt{11}}{6})(-\\frac{3\\sqrt{5}}{7})+(\\frac{5}{6})(\\frac{2}{7}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{3\\sqrt{55}}{42}+\\frac{10}{42} \\\\  & =\\frac{10-3\\sqrt{55}}{42}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, $a=10,b=3,$ and $a+b=13.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042307/state.json" "file_sha256" "91de0b72952f6605dfeaab5b348a2225fed004b595bcbad5398beb49a5f2e3ef"} {"question" "q-119451" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042307/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "769f6b658de1e87837e89c0a286c34b86d9a2f842100ee4154533f402cb568f9"} {"question" "q-119451" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042307/state.json" "file_sha256" "91de0b72952f6605dfeaab5b348a2225fed004b595bcbad5398beb49a5f2e3ef"} {"question" "q-119451" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "13"] ["math" "15"] ["math" "16"] ["math" "21"] ["math" "7"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042307/state.json" "file_sha256" "91de0b72952f6605dfeaab5b348a2225fed004b595bcbad5398beb49a5f2e3ef"} {"question" "q-119451" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "13"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042307/state.json" "file_sha256" "91de0b72952f6605dfeaab5b348a2225fed004b595bcbad5398beb49a5f2e3ef"} {"question" "q-119179" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Suppose that for the angles $u$ and $v,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡u=-\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{4},\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡u=\\frac{\\sqrt{11}}{4},\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡v=-\\frac{5}{6}.\n$$\n\nGiven that $v$ lies in the second quadrant, and\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u-v)=\\frac{a\\sqrt{5}-b}{24},\n$$\n\ncalculate the value of $a+b.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042307/state.json" "file_sha256" "91de0b72952f6605dfeaab5b348a2225fed004b595bcbad5398beb49a5f2e3ef"} {"question" "q-119179" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, recall the difference formula for sine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u-v)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v-\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\n$$\n\nWe know the values of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡u$, $\\operatorname{cos}⁡u$, and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡v.$ To find the missing value of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡v$, we can use the Pythagorean identity:\n\n$$\n{sin}^{2}⁡v+{cos}^{2}⁡v=1\\,⟹\\,{sin}^{2}⁡v=1-{cos}^{2}⁡v\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values into the Pythagorean identity, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡v & =1-{cos}^{2}⁡v \\\\  & =1-{(-\\frac{5}{6})}^{2} \\\\  & =1-{(\\frac{5}{6})}^{2} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{{5}^{2}}{{6}^{2}} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{25}{36} \\\\  & =\\frac{36}{36}-\\frac{25}{36} \\\\  & =\\frac{11}{36}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡v & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{11}{36}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{11}}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that $v$ lies in the second quadrant, where sine is positive. So, we disregard the negative value, and we have \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡v=\\frac{\\sqrt{11}}{6}.\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the difference formula, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u-v) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v-\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v \\\\  & =(-\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{4})(-\\frac{5}{6})-(\\frac{\\sqrt{11}}{4})(\\frac{\\sqrt{11}}{6}) \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\sqrt{5}}{24}-\\frac{11}{24} \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\sqrt{5}-11}{24}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, $a=5,b=11,$ and $a+b=16.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042307/state.json" "file_sha256" "91de0b72952f6605dfeaab5b348a2225fed004b595bcbad5398beb49a5f2e3ef"} {"question" "q-119179" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042307/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "769f6b658de1e87837e89c0a286c34b86d9a2f842100ee4154533f402cb568f9"} {"question" "q-119179" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042307/state.json" "file_sha256" "91de0b72952f6605dfeaab5b348a2225fed004b595bcbad5398beb49a5f2e3ef"} {"question" "q-119179" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "10"] ["math" "12"] ["math" "15"] ["math" "16"] ["math" "19"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042307/state.json" "file_sha256" "91de0b72952f6605dfeaab5b348a2225fed004b595bcbad5398beb49a5f2e3ef"} {"question" "q-119179" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "16"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042307/state.json" "file_sha256" "91de0b72952f6605dfeaab5b348a2225fed004b595bcbad5398beb49a5f2e3ef"} {"question" "q-65423" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Suppose that for the angles $u$ and $v,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=-\\frac{4}{5},\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡y=\\frac{12}{13},\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡y=\\frac{5}{13}.\n$$\n\nGiven that $x$ lies in the second quadrant, find the value of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(y-x).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042307/state.json" "file_sha256" "91de0b72952f6605dfeaab5b348a2225fed004b595bcbad5398beb49a5f2e3ef"} {"question" "q-65423" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, recall the difference formula for sine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡(y-x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\n$$\n\nWe know the values of $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$, $\\operatorname{sin}⁡y$, and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡y.$ To find the missing value of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$, we can use the Pythagorean identity:\n\n$$\n{sin}^{2}⁡x+{cos}^{2}⁡x=1\\,⟹\\,{sin}^{2}⁡x=1-{cos}^{2}⁡x\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values into the Pythagorean identity, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡x & =1-{cos}^{2}⁡x \\\\  & =1-{(-\\frac{4}{5})}^{2} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{(-4)^{2}}{{5}^{2}} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{16}{25} \\\\  & =\\frac{25}{25}-\\frac{16}{25} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{25}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{9}{25}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{3}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that $x$ lies in the second quadrant, where sine is positive. So, we disregard the negative value, and we have \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=\\frac{3}{5}.\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the difference formula, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(y-x) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\  & =(\\frac{12}{13})(-\\frac{4}{5})-(\\frac{5}{13})(\\frac{3}{5}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{48}{65}-\\frac{15}{65} \\\\  & =-\\frac{63}{65}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042307/state.json" "file_sha256" "91de0b72952f6605dfeaab5b348a2225fed004b595bcbad5398beb49a5f2e3ef"} {"question" "q-65423" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042307/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "769f6b658de1e87837e89c0a286c34b86d9a2f842100ee4154533f402cb568f9"} {"question" "q-65423" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042307/state.json" "file_sha256" "91de0b72952f6605dfeaab5b348a2225fed004b595bcbad5398beb49a5f2e3ef"} {"question" "q-65423" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-\\frac{23}{65}"] ["math" "-\\frac{49}{65}"] ["math" "-\\frac{63}{65}"] ["math" "\\frac{13}{65}"] ["math" "\\frac{33}{65}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042307/state.json" "file_sha256" "91de0b72952f6605dfeaab5b348a2225fed004b595bcbad5398beb49a5f2e3ef"} {"question" "q-65423" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-\\frac{63}{65}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042307/state.json" "file_sha256" "91de0b72952f6605dfeaab5b348a2225fed004b595bcbad5398beb49a5f2e3ef"} {"question" "q-144398" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Suppose that for the angles $x$ and $y,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=-\\frac{7}{25},\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡y=\\frac{3}{5},\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡y=-\\frac{4}{5}.\n$$\n\nGiven that $x$ lies in the fourth quadrant, find the value of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(y-x).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042307/state.json" "file_sha256" "91de0b72952f6605dfeaab5b348a2225fed004b595bcbad5398beb49a5f2e3ef"} {"question" "q-144398" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, recall the difference formula for sine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡(y-x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\n$$\n\nWe know the values of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$, $\\operatorname{sin}⁡y$, and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡y.$ To find the missing value of $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$, we can use the Pythagorean identity:\n\n$$\n{sin}^{2}⁡x+{cos}^{2}⁡x=1\\,⟹\\,{cos}^{2}⁡x=1-{sin}^{2}⁡x\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values into the Pythagorean identity, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{cos}^{2}⁡x & =1-{sin}^{2}⁡x \\\\  & =1-{(-\\frac{7}{25})}^{2} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{(-7)^{2}}{{25}^{2}} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{49}{625} \\\\  & =\\frac{625}{625}-\\frac{49}{625} \\\\  & =\\frac{576}{625}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{576}{625}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{24}{25}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that $x$ lies in the fourth quadrant, where cosine is positive. So, we disregard the negative value, and we have \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=\\frac{24}{25}.\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the difference formula, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(y-x) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\  & =(\\frac{3}{5})(\\frac{24}{25})-(-\\frac{4}{5})(-\\frac{7}{25}) \\\\  & =\\frac{72}{125}-\\frac{28}{125} \\\\  & =\\frac{44}{125}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042307/state.json" "file_sha256" "91de0b72952f6605d