y/question-capture-workers/differential/14042307/state.json" "file_sha256" "91de0b72952f6605dfeaab5b348a2225fed004b595bcbad5398beb49a5f2e3ef"} {"question" "q-274544" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, recall the difference formula for sine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u-v)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v-\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\n$$\n\nWe know the values of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡v$, $\\operatorname{cos}⁡v$, and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡u.$ To find the missing value of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡u$, we can use the Pythagorean identity:\n\n$$\n{sin}^{2}⁡u+{cos}^{2}⁡u=1\\,⟹\\,{sin}^{2}⁡u=1-{cos}^{2}⁡u\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values into the Pythagorean identity, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡u & =1-{cos}^{2}⁡u \\\\  & =1-{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{2} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{{1}^{2}}{{3}^{2}} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{1}{9} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{9}-\\frac{1}{9} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡u & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{7}{9}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that $u$ lies in the fourth quadrant, where sine is negative. So, we disregard the positive value, and we have \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡u=-\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{3}.\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the difference formula, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u-v) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v-\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v \\\\  & =(-\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{3})(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})-(\\frac{1}{3})(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{4}{6}+\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}-4}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042307/state.json" "file_sha256" "91de0b72952f6605dfeaab5b348a2225fed004b595bcbad5398beb49a5f2e3ef"} {"question" "q-274544" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042307/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "769f6b658de1e87837e89c0a286c34b86d9a2f842100ee4154533f402cb568f9"} {"question" "q-274544" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042307/state.json" "file_sha256" "91de0b72952f6605dfeaab5b348a2225fed004b595bcbad5398beb49a5f2e3ef"} {"question" "q-274544" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}-4}{6}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042307/state.json" "file_sha256" "91de0b72952f6605dfeaab5b348a2225fed004b595bcbad5398beb49a5f2e3ef"} {"question" "q-326378" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Suppose that for the angles $u$ and $v,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡u=\\frac{7}{25},\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡u=\\frac{24}{25},\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡v=-\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nGiven that $v$ lies in the second quadrant, then\n\n| $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u-v)$ | $=$ | {{field-1}}$\\,\\,-\\,$ {{field-2}}$\\sqrt{3}$ | . |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  |  | $50$ |  |" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042307/state.json" "file_sha256" "91de0b72952f6605dfeaab5b348a2225fed004b595bcbad5398beb49a5f2e3ef"} {"question" "q-326378" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we recall the difference formula for sine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u-v)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v-\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\n$$\n\nWe know the values of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡u$, $\\operatorname{cos}⁡u$, and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡v.$ To find the missing value of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡v,$ we can use the Pythagorean identity:\n\n$$\n{sin}^{2}⁡v+{cos}^{2}⁡v=1\\,⟹\\,{sin}^{2}⁡v=1-{cos}^{2}⁡v\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values into the Pythagorean identity, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡v & =1-{cos}^{2}⁡v \\\\  & =1-{(-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{{1}^{2}}{{2}^{2}} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{4}-\\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡v & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{3}{4}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that $v$ lies in the second quadrant, where sine is positive. So, we disregard the negative value, and we have \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡v=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the difference formula, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u-v) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v-\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v \\\\  & =(\\frac{7}{25})(-\\frac{1}{2})-(\\frac{24}{25})(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{7}{50}-\\frac{24\\sqrt{3}}{50} \\\\  & =\\frac{-7-24\\sqrt{3}}{50}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042307/state.json" "file_sha256" "91de0b72952f6605dfeaab5b348a2225fed004b595bcbad5398beb49a5f2e3ef"} {"question" "q-326378" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042307/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "769f6b658de1e87837e89c0a286c34b86d9a2f842100ee4154533f402cb568f9"} {"question" "q-326378" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042307/state.json" "file_sha256" "91de0b72952f6605dfeaab5b348a2225fed004b595bcbad5398beb49a5f2e3ef"} {"question" "q-326378" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-7"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042307/state.json" "file_sha256" "91de0b72952f6605dfeaab5b348a2225fed004b595bcbad5398beb49a5f2e3ef"} {"question" "q-326378" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042307/state.json" "file_sha256" "91de0b72952f6605dfeaab5b348a2225fed004b595bcbad5398beb49a5f2e3ef"} {"question" "q-326378" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "24"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042307/state.json" "file_sha256" "91de0b72952f6605dfeaab5b348a2225fed004b595bcbad5398beb49a5f2e3ef"} {"question" "e-3520" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Suppose that for the angles $u$ and $v,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\sin u = \\dfrac{3}{4}, \\qquad \\cos u =  -\\dfrac{\\sqrt{7}}{4}, \\qquad \\cos v = \\dfrac{4}{5}.\n$$\n\nGiven that $v$ lies in the first quadrant, and\n\n$$\n\\sin\\left(u + v\\right) = \\dfrac{a- b\\sqrt 7}{20},\n$$\n\ncalculate the value of $a+b.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042307/example-3520.json" "file_sha256" "1a090c74ed66ea4d032ff13c3b1f5efd20176adb4612541bcc342e9102901314"} {"question" "e-3520" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, recall the sum formula for sine:\n\n$$\n\\sin(u+v) = \\sin u \\cos v+ \\cos u \\sin v\n$$\n\nWe know the values of $\\sin{u},$ $\\cos{u},$ and $\\cos{v}.$ To find the missing value of $\\sin{v},$ we can use the Pythagorean identity:\n\n$$\n\\sin^2 v + \\cos^2 v= 1 \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad \\sin^2 v = 1- \\cos^2 v\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values into the Pythagorean identity, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡v & =1-{cos}^{2}⁡v \\\\  & =1-{(\\frac{4}{5})}^{2} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{{4}^{2}}{{5}^{2}} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{16}{25} \\\\  & =\\frac{25}{25}-\\frac{16}{25} \\\\  & =\\frac{25-16}{25} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{25}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡v & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{9}{25}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{3}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that $v$ lies in the first quadrant, where sine is positive. So, we disregard the negative value, and we have \n\n$$\n\\sin v= \\dfrac{3}{5}.\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the sum formula, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u+v) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v+\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v \\\\  & =(\\frac{3}{4})(\\frac{4}{5})+(-\\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{4})(\\frac{3}{5}) \\\\  & =\\frac{12}{20}-\\frac{3\\sqrt{7}}{20} \\\\  & =\\frac{12-3\\sqrt{7}}{20}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, $a=12, b=3,$ and $a+b = 15.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042307/example-3520.json" "file_sha256" "1a090c74ed66ea4d032ff13c3b1f5efd20176adb4612541bcc342e9102901314"} {"question" "e-9984" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Suppose that for the angles $u$ and $v,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\cos u = \\dfrac{7}{25}, \\qquad \\sin v = \\dfrac{4}{5}, \\qquad \\cos v = \\dfrac {3} {5}.\n$$\n\nGiven that $u$ lies in the fourth quadrant, find the value of $\\sin(u-v).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042307/example-9984.json" "file_sha256" "59eb469dbae70027f6ef79c1bd9eb253c0ba41d4aaa94df725fc9f182ab187bc"} {"question" "e-9984" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, recall the difference formula for sine:\n\n$$\n\\sin(u-v) = \\sin u \\cos v- \\cos u \\sin v\n$$\n\nWe know the values of $\\cos{u}$, $\\sin{v}$, and $\\cos{v}.$ To find the missing value of $\\sin{u}$, we can use the Pythagorean identity:\n\n$$\n\\sin^2 u + \\cos^2 u= 1 \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad \\sin^2 u = 1- \\cos^2 u\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values into the Pythagorean identity, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡u & =1-{cos}^{2}⁡u \\\\  & =1-{(\\frac{7}{25})}^{2} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{{7}^{2}}{{25}^{2}} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{49}{625} \\\\  & =\\frac{625}{625}-\\frac{49}{625} \\\\  & =\\frac{576}{625}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡u & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{576}{625}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{24}{25}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that $u$ lies in the fourth quadrant, where sine is negative. So, we disregard the positive value, and we have \n\n$$\n\\sin u= -\\dfrac{24}{25}.\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the difference formula, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\t\\sin(u - v) \n\t\t\t&= \\sin u \\cos v - \\cos u \\sin v \\[5pt]\n\t\t\t&= \\left( -\\dfrac {24}{25} \\right)\\left(\\dfrac {3} {5}\\right)\n \t\t\t\t -  \\left(\\dfrac {7}{25}\\right) \\left( \\dfrac {4} {5}\\right) \\[5pt]\n\t\t\t\n \t\t\t&= -\\dfrac {72} {125} -  \\dfrac {28} {125} \\[5pt]\n\t\t\t&= -\\dfrac {100} {125}\\[5pt]\n\t\t\t&= -\\dfrac {4} {5}.\n\t}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042307/example-9984.json" "file_sha256" "59eb469dbae70027f6ef79c1bd9eb253c0ba41d4aaa94df725fc9f182ab187bc"}] "usage" {"q-119179" [] "q-65423" [] "e-3520" [] "e-9984" []} "basis" 1551 "retractions" [[17592186184396 :question/problem "Suppose that for the angles $u$ and $v$, we have\n$\\sin u =-\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{4}, \\cos u = \\frac{\\sqrt{11}}{4}, \\cos v =-\\frac{5}{6}$.\n\nGiven that $v$ lies in the second quadrant, and\n$\\sin (u - v) = \\frac{a\\sqrt{5} - b}{24}$,\n\ncalculate the value of $a + b$."] [17592186184768 :question/problem "Suppose that for the angles $u$ and $v$, we have\n$\\cos x =-\\frac{4}{5}, \\sin y = \\frac{12}{13}, \\cos y = \\frac{5}{13}$.\n\nGiven that $x$ lies in the second quadrant, find the value of $\\sin (y - x)$."] [17592186183996 :question/problem "Suppose that for the angles $u$ and $v$, we have\n\n$\\sin u = \\dfrac{3}{4}, \\qquad \\cos u = -\\dfrac{\\sqrt{7}}{4}, \\qquad \\cos v = \\dfrac{4}{5}$.\n\nGiven that $v$ lies in the first quadrant, and\n$\\sin\\left(u + v\\right) = \\dfrac{a- b\\sqrt 7}{20}$,\n\ncalculate the value of $a+b$."] [17592186183996 :question/worked-solution "First, recall the sum formula for sine:\n\n$$\n\\sin(u+v) = \\sin u \\cos v+ \\cos u \\sin v\n$$\n\nWe know the values of $\\sin{u}$, $\\cos{u}$, and $\\cos{v}$. To find the missing value of $\\sin{v}$, we can use the Pythagorean identity:\n\n$$\n\\sin^2 v + \\cos^2 v= 1 \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad \\sin^2 v = 1- \\cos^2 v\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values into the Pythagorean identity, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\sin^{2} v &= 1 - \\cos^{2} v \\\\\n&= 1 - (\\frac{4}{5})^{2} \\\\\n&= 1 - \\frac{4^{2}}{5^{2}} \\\\\n&= 1 - \\frac{16}{25} \\\\\n&= \\frac{25}{25} - \\frac{16}{25} \\\\\n&= \\frac{25 - 16}{25} \\\\\n&= \\frac{9}{25}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\sin v &= ± \\sqrt{\\frac{9}{25}} \\\\\n&= ± \\frac{3}{5}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that $v$ lies in the first quadrant, where sine is positive. So, we disregard the negative value, and we have\n\n$$\n\\sin v= \\dfrac{3}{5}\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the sum formula, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\sin (u + v) &= \\sin u\\cos v + \\cos u\\sin v \\\\\n&= (\\frac{3}{4})(\\frac{4}{5}) + (-\\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{4})(\\frac{3}{5}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{12}{20} - \\frac{3\\sqrt{7}}{20} \\\\\n&= \\frac{12 - 3\\sqrt{7}}{20}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, $a=12, b=3$, and\n\n$$\na+b = 15\n$$"] [17592186184076 :question/problem "Suppose that for the angles $u$ and $v$, we have\n$\\cos u = \\dfrac{7}{25}, \\qquad \\sin v = \\dfrac{4}{5}, \\qquad \\cos v = \\dfrac {3} {5}$.\n\nGiven that $u$ lies in the fourth quadrant, find the value of $\\sin(u-v)$."] [17592186184076 :question/worked-solution "First, recall the difference formula for sine:\n\n$$\n\\sin(u-v) = \\sin u \\cos v- \\cos u \\sin v\n$$\n\nWe know the values of $\\cos{u}$, $\\sin{v}$, and $\\cos{v}$. To find the missing value of $\\sin{u}$, we can use the Pythagorean identity:\n\n$$\n\\sin^2 u + \\cos^2 u= 1 \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad \\sin^2 u = 1- \\cos^2 u\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values into the Pythagorean identity, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\sin^{2} u &= 1 - \\cos^{2} u \\\\\n&= 1 - (\\frac{7}{25})^{2} \\\\\n&= 1 - \\frac{7^{2}}{25^{2}} \\\\\n&= 1 - \\frac{49}{625} \\\\\n&= \\frac{625}{625} - \\frac{49}{625} \\\\\n&= \\frac{576}{625}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\sin u &= ± \\sqrt{\\frac{576}{625}} \\\\\n&= ± \\frac{24}{25}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that $u$ lies in the fourth quadrant, where sine is negative. So, we disregard the positive value, and we have\n\n$$\n\\sin u= -\\dfrac{24}{25}\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the difference formula, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} \\sin(u - v) &= \\sin u \\cos v - \\cos u \\sin v \\\\[5pt] &= \\left(-\\dfrac {24}{25} \\right)\\left(\\dfrac {3} {5}\\right) - \\left(\\dfrac {7}{25}\\right) \\left(\\dfrac {4} {5}\\right) \\\\[5pt] &= -\\dfrac {72} {125} - \\dfrac {28} {125} \\\\[5pt] &= -\\dfrac {100} {125} \\\\[5pt] &= -\\dfrac {4} {5}. \\end{aligned}\n$$"]] "immutable_answers" [17592186180826 17592186180827 17592186180828 17592186180829 17592186180830 17592186183498 17592186183499 17592186183500 17592186183501 17592186183502] "immutable_fields" [[17592186180825 :answer-field/id] [17592186180825 :answer-field/key] [17592186180825 :answer-field/type] [17592186183497 :answer-field/id] [17592186183497 :answer-field/key] [17592186183497 :answer-field/type]] "no_history"